            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

As discussed, your house has some excess air leakage that is coming from a few key areas:



** The perimeter wall trim on the 2nd floor - most noticeable in the front right side room where the sloped ceiling meets the flat ceiling and from the location of the wood stove chimney chase.



** The attic access panel in the front addition log room, as well as the attic access panel on the 2nd floor. These can be sealed with a removeable caulking such as Draft Attack



** Through the woodstove itself, as there is currently not a dedicated outside combustion air supply directly connected and sealed to the back of your Blaze King Princess. This wood stove is therefore drawing your heated air from inside to burn &amp; then sending it up the chimney .... which requires makeup air to leak into your house wherever there are cracks &amp; holes.



** The access hole through the right siide of your log house (likely used to load wood into the house, without the need to carry it through from the front door).



** Around the older windows &amp; doors, especially the skylight in the main floor bathroom



Savings: There should be a minimum 13% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing that could potentially save $ 441 per year in energy costs (about $118 of this is associated with connecting a dedicated outside combustion air supply to your Blaze King Princess), decrease your annual energy usage by 10.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <windows>

You were curious as to what the energy savings would be if you were to upgrade all 14 of the windows (including the 2 x skylights) to new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. The current wood windows are double pane with no Low E coating or Argon Gas Fill, and many have dates stamped from 1985 (about 38 years old) and the large window upstairs is date stamped from 1971. Even though they are older, most of them are reasonably well sealed when closed &amp; latched. 



If you do proceed to upgrade any or all of them, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K. For your house, I modeled more efficient windows with a U-Value no higher than 1.05 W/mÂ²K.



Savings:

By replacing all of the windows in your house with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 878 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $325 per window (up to $4,550 for all 14 windows) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage</windows>
                <doors>

In case you may consider upgrading any of the current exterior doors with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.



Savings:

As an example, I modeled replacing a front door of the 2nd floor with one that meets the criteria listed above ..... which is estimated to save $ 9 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $162.50 in rebates (1 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank, located in the rear heated outbuilding and supplying hot water (through the mini utilidor) to your house. You mentioned that you may want to bring your domestic water supply tank and the hot water heater inside the house. As electricity is quite expensive in Norman Wells and this electric hot water tank is essentially a large "kettle", I have modeled the opportunity to replace the hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher (eg: Toyotomi OM-122DW).



Savings: By replacing your current electric hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher, could potentially save $ 845 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

FIRST THING - The "Green Box" introduction to this section indicates that I am recommending you install a new oil boiler &amp; 2 new biomass heating units - this is just a glitch in the reporting software that "auto fills" that box. As you will see below, I am only talking about installing a wood pellet boiler to help reduce oil boiler fuel use.



Your house is currently heated with a Weil McLain WTGO-4 oil boiler that is rated at 85.0% efficiency and is located in the shop behind your house and connects to the home heat distribution systems via a mini utilidor. You mentioned that heating with oil is very expensive, so you will use your wood stove regularly to help reduce cost.



We talked about was an opportunity for you to add a wood pellet boiler similar to others installed in Norman Wells at both commercial buildings (airport, hospital, other) and even residential sized boilers that have been in continuous use as the primary heating system for many years. I do not recommend any one manufacturer or brand, but I can share that the residential unit I saw last month in Norman Wells was a Maine Energy Systems AutoPellet S32. I'm sure you can ask around town as to which company is installing these systems and ask to talk with existing customers regarding their level of satisfaction.



I have modeled an option for you to install a high efficiency wood pellet boiler, with a minimum 85% efficiency that is sized to meet 100% of the heat load of your house, and hopefully your domestic hot water as well. The current oil boiler will become a backup (for very cold weather supplemental heating).



Savings: By installing a high efficiency wood pellet boiler, with a minimum 85% efficiency that is sized to meet 100% of the heat load of your house, you could potentially save $ 2,188 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 7.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program.



For more information and pre-approval rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/



NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



The Arctic Energy Alliance administers the Alternative Energy Technologies Program (AETP) that includes rebates for Solar PV systems, including integrated battery energy storage for NWT residents who are not connected to the hydro-electric grid. 



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the right facing roof (Southeast) of the 2nd story of your home &amp; directly connected to the Norman Wells grid (ie: not including a battery storage system). This is estimated to produce about 3,578 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,241, and qualifying for up to $20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program.



For more information and pre-approval rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/



NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



Canada Greener Homes Grant 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ESTIMAED ENERGY COST SAVINGS - USING YOUR WOOD STOVE



You mentioned that you harvest wood and use your Blaze King Princess to help reduce the heating requirements of the oil boiler. Based on the location of65% of the total floor area (main + 2nd floor combined) and I'll assume you use the wood stove for about 50% of the annual heating load for this space. The Arctic Energy Alliance assigns a nominal cost of $100/cord for self-harvested wood (amortized purchase &amp; use of a chainsaw + the safety gear and transportation costs).



Savings: I modeled the estimated annual savings if you were to use your 80% efficient wood stove to offset 50% of the heating load for about 65% of the area of your house. The estimated savings ($100/cord of wood, less the cost of reduced oil use for the current boiler) is estimated as $3,860/year.



----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Norman Wells, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house,1 bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 0.9 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler is located in an outbuilding (so not affected by this), however the woodstove is chimney vented &amp; is not at current risk of back-drafting, however I still recommend you install a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe - directly connected to the air intake at the back of the stove.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood - only recirculates inside the house.

        - 1 bathroom fan (lower main floor bathroom)

        - The clothes dryer



If you decide to replace the range hood or bathroom fans, please choose an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Norman Wells



Within this report, the Arctic Energy Alliance uses the following Dec. 1, 2022 values (excluding GST) to calculate energy cost savings for your home in Norman Wells:



Electricity - $18.00 + $0.13 per kWh up to the monthly subsidy threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter

Oil**  - $1.61 per litre 

Wood** - $588 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets** - $8.44 per bag; $594.60 per tonne bulk delivered



** Oil &amp; Biomass fuels in Norman Wells tend to experience cyclical fuel price volatility. The Arctic Energy Alliance therefore uses a 5-year rolling average for these values to better help you evaluate longer term heating appliance purchase/upgrade decisions.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



